{"id": "section-body-09191", "title": "Bleeding of Obscure Origin", "chapterId": "chapter-ten", "tocLevel": 4, "tocPath": ["APPROACH TO PATIENTS WITH EMERGENCY CONDITIONS", "GASTROINTESTINAL DISEASE CONDITIONS", "Disorders of Gastrointestinal Tract", "Bleeding of Obscure Origin"], "blocks": [{"id": "body-09191", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading4", "text": "Bleeding of Obscure Origin", "runs": [{"text": "Bleeding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Obscure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Origin"}], "level": 4, "list": {"level": 3, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09192", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "Obscure GI bleeding is recurrent blood loss without an identified source of bleeding despite upper endoscopy and colonoscopy. Patients aged ≤50 years are more likely to have tumors (leiomyomas, carcinoid, adenocarcinoma, or lymphoma), Dieulafoylesion, or Crohn’s disease. Older patients are more likely to have vascular lesions, such as angiodysplasia. Angiodysplasia is the most common cause of obscure GI bleeding overall (40% of all cases). Patients may present with either melena or hematochezia or positive fecal occult blood test (FOBT). The first step is to repeat upper endoscopy and/or colonoscopy, which is diagnostic in approximately 40% of cases.", "runs": [{"text": "Obscure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "GI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bleeding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "recurrent"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "blood"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "loss"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "without"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "an"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "identified"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "source"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bleeding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "despite"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "upper"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endoscopy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "colonoscopy. Patients aged ≤50 years are more likely to have tumors (leiomyomas, carcinoid, adenocarcinoma, or lymphoma), Dieulafoylesion, or Crohn’s disease. Older patients are more likely to have vascular lesions, such as angiodysplasia. Angiodysplasia"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "most"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "common"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cause"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "obscure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "GI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bleeding"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "overall"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(40%"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "all"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "cases)."}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "may"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "present"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "either melena or hematochezia or positive fecal occult blood test (FOBT). The first step is to repeat upper endoscopy and/or colonoscopy, which is diagnostic in approximately 40% of cases."}]}, {"id": "body-09194", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "For patients with obscure active GI bleeding", "runs": [{"text": "For"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "obscure"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "active"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "GI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bleeding"}]}, {"id": "body-09195", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Nuclear studies (technetium 99m-labeled erythrocyte or sulfur colloid nuclear scan) first, followed by angiography", "runs": [{"text": "Nuclear"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "studies"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(technetium"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "99m-labeled"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "erythrocyte"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "sulfur"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "colloid"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "nuclear"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "scan)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "first,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "followed"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "by"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "angiography"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09196", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "if unrevealing, consider push enteroscopy or balloon-assisted enteroscopy (deep enteroscopy)", "runs": [{"text": "if"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "unrevealing,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "consider"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "push"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "enteroscopy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "balloon-assisted"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "enteroscopy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(deep"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "enteroscopy)"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09197", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Surgery and intraoperative enteroscopy is a last diagnostic option", "runs": [{"text": "Surgery"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "and"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "intraoperative"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "enteroscopy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "is"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "a"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "last"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "diagnostic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "option"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09199", "type": "paragraph", "style": "BodyText", "text": "For patients with occult GI bleeding", "runs": [{"text": "For"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "patients"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "with"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "occult"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "GI"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "bleeding"}]}, {"id": "body-09200", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "Capsule endoscopy (first choice GIE 2015; 81:889) or deep enteroscopy", "runs": [{"text": "Capsule"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endoscopy"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(first"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "choice"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "GIE"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "2015;"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "81:889)"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "deep"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "enteroscopy"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09201", "type": "paragraph", "style": "ListParagraph", "text": "If unrevealing, repeat endoscopic examinations (upper endoscopy, colonoscopy, capsule endoscopy), or deep enteroscopy", "runs": [{"text": "If"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "unrevealing,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "repeat"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endoscopic"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "examinations"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "(upper"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endoscopy,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "colonoscopy,"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "capsule"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "endoscopy),"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "or"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "deep "}, {"text": "enteroscopy"}], "list": {"level": 4, "numberingId": "1503"}}, {"id": "body-09203", "type": "heading", "style": "Heading2", "text": "Diseases of the Intestine", "runs": [{"text": "Diseases"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "of"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "the"}, {"text": " "}, {"text": "Intestine"}], "level": 2, "list": {"level": 1, "numberingId": "1531"}}], "source": {"file": "medical.docx", "chapter": "Gastrointestinal disease conditions", "start": "body-09191", "end": "body-09203"}}